<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Musha'sha</h1><p class="st-meta">1436 CE – 1729 CE (approx.) · capital Hoveyzeh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A messianic Shia Arab dynasty of Khuzestan, founded by a self-proclaimed Mahdi, that later ruled 'Arabistan' as Safavid vassals for two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-falah">Muhammad ibn Falah</a>, a scholar from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasit">Wasit</a> trained in Shia theology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilla">Hilla</a>, proclaimed himself the representative of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Hidden Imam</a>, or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> himself, in the 1430s. Gathering Arab tribes of the marshes, he founded a state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> around 1436 with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoveyzeh">Hoveyzeh</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The early Musha'sha held extreme (ghulat) Shia beliefs: Muhammad's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawla-ali">Mawla Ali</a> claimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a> was divine and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a> in 1453. The state rested on Arab tribal warriors and the date and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karun-river">Karun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karkheh-river">Karkheh</a> rivers. After Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> in 1508, the Musha'sha became walis (governors) of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-mohammerah">Arabistan</a> under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> and moved towards conventional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Musha'sha declined in the 18th century amid Afghan and Ottoman invasions after the Safavid collapse. By 1729 real power had passed to other tribes, notably the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banu-kab-sheikhdom">Banu Ka'b sheikhdom</a>, though Musha'sha sayyids kept a local role at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoveyzeh">Hoveyzeh</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mushasha.jpg" alt="Musha'sha" width="320" height="242"><figcaption>Image: Ahmadf.alabbasi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D9%88%D9%84%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B4%D8%B9%D8%B4%D8%B9%D9%8A%D8%A92.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1436 CE – 1729 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hoveyzeh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Falah, Mawla Ali, Sayyid Mubarak</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Musha'sha'iyyah, Musha'sha dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1582&amp;focus=mushasha" aria-label="Musha'sha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5921 1032 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Musha'sha lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6241 1288L6153 1065M6241 1288L6281 1235M6241 1288L6298 1144"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6153 1065h0M6281 1235h0M6298 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6241 1288h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6241 1288h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hoveyzeh. <a href="/atlas?year=1582&amp;focus=mushasha">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

